{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS005271","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All three candidates are direct matches to the target trait: each has trait_efo = "Hashimoto's thyroiditis" and phenotyping_reported = "lymphocytic thyroiditis" (a synonymous clinical label for Hashimoto’s), with no explicit endpoint mismatch. They are from the same publication family (medRxiv preprint) with the same validation sample size (n=94,651) and similar multi-ancestry evaluation context, so those factors do not differentiate them meaningfully.

Between the two PRSCS genome-wide scores (PGS005271, PGS005272) and the P+T score (PGS005270), domain guidance favors genome-wide shrinkage methods when endpoints are aligned. Among the PRSCS siblings, PGS005271 has the strongest visible discrimination/effect evidence (full-model AUROC 0.6297 vs 0.6054; OR per SD 1.549 vs 1.417), so it is the best-supported choice within this coherent direct-match cluster.

Limitation: the only reported AUROC appears to be a full-model AUROC (no PRS-only AUC/R2), and covariates are listed as "Unknown" for all models, so cross-model ranking confidence is constrained and the selection is necessarily sub-optimal despite being the most supported among available direct matches."}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY",
  "best_model_id": "PGS004911",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All four candidates are direct matches to hypertrophic cardiomyopathy at the trait/efo level, but PGS000778 has a notable endpoint shift (phenotyping is clinical events among pathogenic sarcomeric variant carriers), making it less aligned with generic HCM risk. PGS000739 reports a high full-model AUROC (0.821) but uses heavy clinical covariates (multiple comorbidities plus rare variant carrier status), so its AUROC is likely covariate-driven and not comparable as standalone PRS evidence. PGS004910 and PGS004911 are from the same disease-focused Nat Genet 2025 evaluation paper, have clean/basic covariates (age, age^2, sex, PC1-10), consistent EUR GWAS/EVAL, large validation (n=343,182), and report full-model R² plus effect sizes; between them, the MTAG version (PGS004911) is mildly preferred per provided domain guidance and also shows stronger reported metrics (AUROC 0.8 vs 0.73; R² 0.048 vs 0.031; HR 2.34 vs 1.97). Confidence is moderated because the reported AUROC values are full-model (not explicitly PRS-only), limiting strict comparability."
}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS005220","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Direct-match candidates are present for the target trait “sleep apnea”, including multiple exact-label “Sleep apnea” models (PGS003204–PGS003218) and several clinically dominant subtype models for “obstructive sleep apnea” (OSA) (PGS003479, PGS003857, PGS003858, PGS005219, PGS005220). Per the provided domain knowledge, OSA is the dominant clinical form of sleep apnea, so OSA-labeled models are acceptable direct matches and can be preferred when better supported.

Among the OSA candidates, PGS005220 is best supported by the visible evidence: it comes from a disease-focused OSA PRS publication (“Polygenic scores for obstructive sleep apnoea…”), uses a multi-score aggregation approach (“weighted PRSsummation PRS-CSs”), and reports strong discriminative effect size (OR=1.53, 95% CI 1.39–1.68) with full-model AUROC=0.79. Compared with the ExPRSweb “Sleep apnea” cluster (PGS003204–PGS003218), which appears to be a pan-trait/framework source and shows near-chance full-model AUROC (~0.49–0.53), PGS005220 has materially stronger PRS-relevant evidence.

Main limitation: the reported AUROC is a full-model metric with covariates including BMI (and race/ethnicity), so the high AUROC may be covariate-inflated and is not directly comparable to PRS-only performance; additionally, PGS005219 (AdjustedBMI) shows an even stronger OR (1.98) but differs in BMI-adjustment framing, making the “best” choice for generic sleep-apnea deployment somewhat ambiguous. Given these comparability uncertainties, the selection is direct-match but not high-confidence."}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS005259",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All listed candidates are direct matches to thyroid carcinoma via trait_efo=thyroid carcinoma (with minor spelling noise in some). Among the strongest endpoint-aligned options, PGS005259 comes from a large, disease-focused multi-ancestry thyroid cancer study with broad multi-biobank development cohorts (e.g., AllofUs, BBJ, BioMe, FinnGen, UKB, MVP, deCODE) and uses a genome-wide Bayesian shrinkage method (PRSCS; 1,085,173 variants), which domain knowledge prioritizes over sparse genome-wide-significant SNP sets when endpoints are comparably aligned. Its reported effect size (OR≈2.04) and AUROC≈0.695 provide supportive evidence without relying on disclosed heavy clinical covariates (covariates are 'Unknown', which is a confidence penalty but not worse than explicit family-history or survivor/treatment-restricted packaging seen in several alternatives). Limitation: the pool contains multiple strong direct-match competitors with good-looking full-model AUROCs (e.g., PGS000208 AUROC=0.751; PGS001354 AUROC=0.83) but these are full-model metrics with additional covariates and/or restricted survivor endpoints, making cross-model comparability uncertain; additionally, PGS005259 is from a medRxiv preprint and has unknown covariates, so while structurally preferred, the evidence is not strong enough to declare a single unambiguous best model at high confidence."
}
